 v2.5.9 adds: Ookla speedtest to Suggests — it powers the guided SQM line-
 speed test but ships neither in the Ubuntu archives nor the TuxWall repo
 (snap or Ookla's apt repo), so it must stay out of Depends; the dashboard
 keeps its availability detection with install hint.
 .
 v2.6.0 fixes the blank attack map and charts after login: the login gate
 hides the whole layout, so the map and every canvas were measured at 0x0
 and never re-measured — Leaflet cached the zero size and each chart's
 small-size guard dropped its frame with no retry, leaving a manual page
 refresh as the only cure. Login now re-measures on the next frame, and a
 ResizeObserver re-draws anything returning from zero size, covering view
 switches too. Also: accessibility and theming pass — prefers-reduced-motion
 support that stops decorative animation while keeping progress spinners
 alive (and without hiding attack-map markers, whose keyframes end at
 opacity 0), light-theme contrast fixes bringing all 8 stat colours and all
 15 nav colours to WCAG AA 4.5:1 (several were as low as 1.9:1), an
 undefined --fg variable that silently broke three rules, a duplicate
 .icon-btn block, and a redrawn Ubuntu mark that no longer vanishes on
 light themes.
 .
 v2.6.1 smooths the CPU, memory and bandwidth graphs. The Catmull-Rom
 control-point coefficient was 0.4 where the maths calls for 1/6, so the
 curve overshot every sample and invented up to ~24px of vertical wobble
 that was never in the data; control points are now also clamped to each
 segment's y-range, which makes overshoot impossible rather than merely
 small. Dense series (300 bandwidth samples across a ~790px card, under
 3px apart) additionally get a centred 3-tap pass, symmetric so peaks keep
 their position in time. Together these cut line travel by ~63% and
 zigzag reversals by ~67%; single-sample peaks are damped by about 5%
 in the drawing only — numeric readouts still show raw samples.
 .
 v2.7.0 is a security release fixing broken access control. The read-only
 "viewer" role was enforced only by hiding controls in the browser: of 101
 POST endpoints just 9 could check role at all, so any signed-in viewer
 could reboot the router, rewrite firewall rules, read WireGuard peer
 configs, restore backups, or drive the AI agent — which runs shell
 commands and holds sudo — while also being able to approve the agent's
 own permission prompts. Role gating is now default-deny: admin is
 required unless an endpoint is explicitly listed, viewers keep only
 self-service password and TOTP actions, and /api/backups/** and
 /api/agent/** are admin-only on GET too (raw backups contain auth.json
 and WireGuard private keys). Also fixes /api/system/self-restart, whose
 loopback check read client_address — always 127.0.0.1 behind nginx
 proxy_pass, leaving it reachable unauthenticated from the LAN or VPN;
 it now resolves the real peer via nginx's X-Real-IP. The AI Assistant
 is hidden from non-admins in the UI to match the server-side rule.
 .
 v2.7.1 fixes the dashboard returning a blank page over IPv6. The nginx
 site listens on [fd00:1::1]:80 but its access lists allowed only
 127.0.0.1, ::1 and the RFC1918 IPv4 ranges, so any client arriving over
 IPv6 hit "deny all" and, via error_page 403 =200, received an empty
 200 rather than an error. It stayed hidden because bare-IP access is
 IPv4-only; it appears as soon as the dashboard is reached by a hostname
 carrying an AAAA record, when browsers prefer IPv6. All four access
 lists now also allow fd00::/8, covering the fd00:1::/64 LAN and the
 fd00:2::/64 WireGuard range. fd00::/8 is used deliberately rather than
 a delegated global prefix, which changes whenever the ISP rotates it.
 Served by nginx from /var/www/html, backed by a Python API server running
 as the systemd unit tuxwall.service on port 8008.
 .
 v2.7.2 adds an Avahi mDNS reflector for Music Assistant / AirPlay /
 Chromecast discovery across segments. mDNS is link-local, so without a
 reflector Home Assistant on one segment cannot discover players on another
 (LAN <-> WireGuard VPN). setup.sh, the first-boot wizard and postinst now
 install avahi-daemon, set enable-reflector=yes under [reflector], deny the
 WAN interface so mDNS never leaks to the internet, and allow UDP 5353
 inbound on LAN/VPN interfaces only. Also: opencode agent gains bounded
 service-control sudo rights (named restarts/reloads, tc, speedtest and
 tuxwall's own scripts — no blanket admin) with matching permission prompts.
